WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.030
- Dear candidate,

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.930
we usually only hire
people with stellar GPAs.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.930
Yours is obviously not that great,

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.770
so why do you think you even
ever chance of working here?

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.500
You're a young graduate,
fresh out of university.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.260
How will you be able to add
value to a senior client?

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.830
You do not seem to have done much

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.970
beyond your academic achievements.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.130
So are you just a boring person?

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.640
Has any of you ever had a
question in an interview

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.020
which really made you uncomfortable?

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.810
So this is something that some people

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.680
like to call a stress interview.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.300
So an interview where
your interview partner

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.060
deliberately tries to
put you under stress.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.230
In this video I will talk about
stress interview situations,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.700
how you can prepare yourself.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:42.810
The framing of this video

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.500
is stress interviews in
job interview situations,

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.130
but of course you might also face

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.110
stress interviews in other instances.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.990
For instance, when you're
facing a hostile client,

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.090
which might ask you questions
that put you under stress.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.200
So welcome to another
video on Firm Learning,

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.530
my name is Heinrich.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.490
I'm a former McKinsey consultant

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:02.240
and on my channel I try to help you

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.460
to become successful in the
first years of your career.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.370
The video will be structured
in the following ways.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.410
So first I will provide a bit of context

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:10.640
on stress interviews.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.380
So do these really happen?

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:13.730
Is this what you need to expect

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.460
when you sit in a consulting interview,

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.780
or maybe also in an interview

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.380
for another type of role or
position in another company.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.080
And then I will share
three tips or reflections

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.110
of how I think about stress interviews

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.180
and what you can do to prepare

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.280
and handle these
situations in the best way.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.510
So let's first talk about the context.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.030
Does this really happen?

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:34.400
Will you need to sit in a stress interview

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.620
when you apply to consulting?

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:36.900
And here, the good thing for you

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.910
is actually that many firms
have policies to not do this.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:44.340
So I know, at least for
Germany, that the MBB firms,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.170
so McKinsey, BCG, and Bain,

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.990
usually do not do job interviews

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.720
for their interview situations.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.590
But of course the truth is
also that interview cultures

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:56.660
differ by region and
of course also by firm.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.570
So it might be that in some firms

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:00.610
you are facing these situations,

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.390
and for sure, outside of consulting,

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.080
there are firms where
this is taking place.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.680
So you better be prepared,
because even for MBB firms,

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.230
often especially the
later stage interviews

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.040
are conducted by partners
or senior partners

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.170
and they do have some flexibility

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.230
of how to conduct these interviews.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.520
So I would never be sure
that this is not something

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.030
that might happen to you as well,

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:24.500
so better be prepared for this.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.720
So what is the why?

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.980
Why am I people decide to do that?

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.360
Why is anybody putting you

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.030
in such a stress situation at all?

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:32.330
And here it might be helpful for you

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.150
just to take a step back.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.120
Think about what these interviews

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.110
are trying to test after all anyway.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.960
And I would argue that
the number one question

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.980
that consulting firms want to
answer when they interview you

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.800
is is this person able
to potentially next week

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.120
be with me on a team at a client?

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.730
Potentially very early on,

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:53.470
in the first one, two, or three weeks,

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:58.023
sit alone with a client, in a
room, having a conversation.

00:02:58.023 --> 00:03:01.440
Will this person be
able to handle it well,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.320
to represent the firm well?

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.900
This is the main question that consultants

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.050
will ask themselves when
interviewing candidates,

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.800
in case interviews, in
personal fit interviews,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.150
and any other conversations

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.560
that are taking place during interviews.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.873
And here, this is just the truth,

00:03:14.873 --> 00:03:16.650
that in a client environment,

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:17.840
if you are a consultant,

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.900
you will face stressful situations

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.673
where clients would try to go after you,

00:03:22.673 --> 00:03:24.440
will try to attack you,

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.310
will try to put you under stress.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.860
Situations like this literally happen

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.030
every single week on most projects.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:35.530
You will regularly sit together
in meetings with clients

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:36.780
where these clients might have

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.390
a different agenda than you have.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.330
Maybe they don't like the project.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:43.430
Maybe they have a general
bias against consultants.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:44.940
Maybe they don't like you.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.090
And they will take
everything that they find

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:49.020
to make you look bad in front of others,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.430
to put you in a stressful situation,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.110
to question your competence,

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:55.040
to question your work results.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.560
And of course, this is not
only the case as a consultant,

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.330
but things like that happen
in almost every company.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.890
This is what is called
politics after all, right?

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:04.087
But I would argue that as a consultant

00:04:04.087 --> 00:04:07.190
you're a little bit more
prone to situations like this

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:08.200
compared to other people

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.410
who are just regular
employees of that company.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:11.970
Now to close the circle, right?

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.800
This is the reason why this
might be tested in interviews.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.680
Interviewer might be
interested to see how you react

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:19.950
if somebody tries to
put you under pressure.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.080
So this is why it's done,

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.070
this is why it's tested.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:25.170
And this is now a great
transition to my three reflections

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.050
and tips on how to handle the situations.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:31.340
And my one tip is to
understand the intention.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.880
Why are people doing this?

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.070
Because many candidates,

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:35.750
if you are in an interview
situation with them,

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.100
and this is happening to them,

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.180
they feel personally attacked.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.060
They feel like, oh, this
interviewer does not like me.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.650
Apparently this interviewer
doesn't want to hire me.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.010
And they instantly get nervous,

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.510
getting defensive, and so on.

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:50.480
But this is of course not the intention

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.210
in almost all of these situations.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.710
In the vast majority
of cases you can assume

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.240
your interviewer to be a
professional after all.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.610
Because if you think about it,

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:00.950
the fact alone that you are
sitting in the interview

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:03.740
means that you passed the
first screening, right?

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:04.920
So the CV screening.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.310
So clearly your CV is considered

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.320
good enough to work in this company.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.380
So if any interviewer is
implying that maybe your GPA,

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:14.790
just to pick this example,

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.400
is not good enough for the job,

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.380
obviously he or she cannot be serious

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:19.840
because otherwise you wouldn't have been

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.210
invited to the interview

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:24.070
if your CV would not have
been good enough on paper.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.040
So tip number one, understand this.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:27.480
This is not about an attack,

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:28.380
but it's a test.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:31.000
Understand the intention
of why this is happening.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.410
Now my second reflection is on what to do.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.100
So how should you respond to that?

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.470
And here I say that even more important

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.470
than what exactly you
say is how you say it.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.620
Many people who get such a question

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:44.860
will instantly turn defensive.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:46.050
They will be nervous,

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:48.820
they suddenly will start
to speak much faster.

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:51.470
Their head will turn
red, and so on, right?

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:54.350
And of course I understand
that this is very difficult,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:56.320
and especially in a stress situation

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.660
than an interview per se already is,

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:00.250
then you're facing such a thing,

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.260
it's really difficult to control yourself.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:03.640
But the first step to improve

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.630
is to at least intellectually understand

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:07.010
what you should avoid.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:09.530
And for sure, do not be defensive.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:11.470
Do not perceive this to be an attack,

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.640
but rather try to
continue the conversation

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.610
in the very same tone of voice,

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.510
in the very same body posture,

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:19.780
just as you behaved before
this question was asked.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:20.870
Because after all,

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:23.750
this is exactly what ideally
you would be able to do

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.730
in front of a client as well.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:27.840
You don't want the
conversation to escalate

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:30.400
into any real direction,
whenever you get a comment

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.230
that potentially catches you off guard.

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:35.030
You want to be perceived as
handling this professionally,

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:38.450
as finding a very calm and
to the point answer to that

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:40.240
and not instantly getting nervous.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:41.940
And this is exactly the confidence

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.130
that you should at least try to radiate.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:45.880
So if you are able to do this

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.640
this is almost already everything

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.230
that then the interviewer
will be looking for.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:50.063
And then of course,

00:06:50.063 --> 00:06:52.500
try to also say a couple
of things that make sense.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.400
Now, again, the GPA example,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.740
when somebody is accusing you

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.600
of the GPA not being good enough.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:58.433
You can just say that,

00:06:58.433 --> 00:07:00.490
yes, you acknowledge that
potentially other people

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:02.000
might have higher GPAs

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.690
but you still believe that you have

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.630
other things that you
can bring to the table.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:08.120
Then maybe you can mention a
couple of other achievements

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.740
in your academic life or
in other areas of your life

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.850
that from your perspective
might compensate for that.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:15.460
Again, I strongly hold
that what exactly you say

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:16.750
isn't even that important,

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.320
but rather how you say it.

