00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.060
Now my third tip is practice,

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.000
because the number one reason

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.710
why usually seasoned
consultants will be quite good

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:52.670
at defending comments like this

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:54.460
in client situations and so on

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:56.330
is not because are geniuses

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:58.320
or super great at this from the beginning,

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.150
but because they already
experienced situations like this

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:03.280
in a very, very frequent way.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.800
And if this happened to you lots of times

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.250
then it's not even a
big deal to you anymore.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:09.730
You know how to handle it,

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.210
you know how to reply.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.800
And the very same thing is
true for job interviews.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:15.900
So if you never thought
that this might happen,

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:17.150
how you would react,

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:19.230
if you've never experienced
anything like this,

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:21.620
of course will be extremely difficult then

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.120
to react and reply in the
right and correct way.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.440
So my tip would be that
you try to expose yourself

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:27.890
to these kinds of situations.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:29.470
So of you still have a couple of years

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:31.100
before you really apply,

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.770
try to get yourself into
a leadership position,

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:35.380
into an operational role,

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:36.650
maybe at an organization,

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:38.940
at a social cause that
you really care about

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:40.230
at your university.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.160
Try to get some things done there.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:43.900
Collect some leadership experience

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:45.200
because in these settings

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.680
you might also face situations like this,

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:49.030
where maybe people attack you,

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:50.830
have another agenda than you.

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:54.250
And this is a great playground
just to develop these skills.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:55.620
If this is now already too late

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:57.370
and now you're really
just a couple of days,

00:09:57.370 --> 00:09:59.210
weeks, or months before the interview,

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:01.400
then maybe try to role
play these situations

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:02.580
with another person,

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.520
where he or she deliberately
asks you questions like this

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:06.960
and then you try to respond.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.020
And then another way how
you can practice this,

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:12.110
probably especially applicable
for introverts like myself,

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:15.610
is rather to play around
these situations in your head.

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:20.360
So try to imagine sitting in
such an interview situation.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:23.030
Think about some questions
that the other person

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:25.830
might ask you that will
really catch you off guard,

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.670
and think about how you would react.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.330
How your blood pressure
and pulse would increase,

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:34.300
how you will feel uneasy,
not really comfortable.

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:35.760
How you would feel scared,

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.010
that maybe this is now
the end of your interview.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.350
Try to really visualize all this

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.880
and then try to relax and think about

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:44.570
how you could find a good answer to that.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.300
I'd argue that if you really mentally

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:49.180
go over these situations in a regular way

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:50.990
this will also potentially help you

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:53.020
to handle these situations all right

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:55.090
in an actual setting in an interview.

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:56.557
So I'd love to hear from you now.

00:10:56.557 --> 00:10:59.670
Did you ever experience
difficult situations like this,

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:00.900
in job interviews

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.550
or when sitting with clients
or other business partners?

